  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1247 AM MDT Tue Aug 4 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nToday there is zonal flow over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This will allow temperatures to warm up a few \ndegrees with dry weather. On Wednesday an upper-level ridge begins \nto build in over North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. \nThis will allow temperatures to warm up some more with dry weather. \nOn Thursday the upper-level ridge remains over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow temperatures to \nwarm back up to well-above seasonal averages. On Wednesday and \nThursday with the increasing temperatures the minimum relative \nhumidity will drop into the teens each afternoon.\n\nOn Friday the upper-level ridge remains over North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. This will allow temperatures to warm up \nfurther and be well-above seasonal averages. Friday \nafternoon/evening the upper-level ridge begins to break down. This \ncombined with strong low-level and mid-level winds will bring windy \nconditions to the area. Afternoon relative humidity will drop into \nthe low teens as well. For more details see the Forecast Confidence \n& Scenarios section. On Saturday upper-level zonal flow is in place \nover North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This will \nkeep warm but slightly cooler temperates over the area. Due to \nstrong mid-level and low-level winds lingering over the area it will \nremain windy on Saturday. On Sunday and Monday there will be \nnorthwest flow aloft which will cool things down slightly to be \ncloser to seasonal averages. It will be mostly dry on Sunday and \nMonday. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFriday through Saturday there is a 30 - 60% chance for wind gusts of \n30 mph or greater across North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana. On Friday and Saturday there is a 30 - 50% chance for \nrelative humidity of 20% or lower and wind speeds of 15 mph or \ngreater across North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. \nClusters and deterministic models are in good agreement that Friday \nafternoon/evening the upper-level ridge will break down and bring \ngusty winds to the area on Friday and Saturday. This combined with \ncritically low relative humidity will lead to at least near critical \nfire weather conditions on Friday and Saturday. Currently \ndeterministic models and ensembles differ on the placement and \ntiming of the strongest upper-level winds and the upper-level ridge \nbreakdown. This affects both how low the relative humidity gets and \nthe strength of the surface winds. \n\nOn Friday there is greater than a 50% chance for high temperatures \nof 95 degrees or greater across most of the North-central Montana \nplans east of I-15. -IG",
